{{wikipedia}}

===Alternative forms===
* [[savoury]] {{qualifier|British}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en-us-savory.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{IPA|/ˈseɪvəri/|lang=en}} 

===Etymology 1===
From the {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|savoure}}, from {{m|fro|savourer}}, from {{etyl|LL.|en}} {{m|la|sapōrāre}}, present active infinitive of {{m|la|sapōrō}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|sapor||taste, flavor}}, from {{m|la|sapio|sapiō, sapere|taste of, have a flavor of}}.

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|savorier}} {{tcx|American spelling}}

# [[tasty|Tasty]], attractive to the [[palate]].
#: ''The fine restaurant presented an array of '''savory''' dishes; each was delicious.''
# [[salty|Salty]] or non-sweet.
#: ''The mushrooms, meat, bread, rice, peanuts and potatoes were all good '''savory''' foods.''
# Not overly [[sweet]].
#: ''The '''savory''' duck contrasted well with the sweet sauce.''
# {{context|figuratively|lang=en}} Morally or ethically [[acceptable]].
#: ''Readers are to be warned that quotations in this chapter contain some not so '''savory''' language.''

